1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:12,835
MATTHEW: Providence, Rhode Island. May of 1842. The smallest state in the union has two governors at the same time, and only one of them was elected by most of the men who wanted to vote.

2
00:00:13,135 --> 00:00:23,168
MATTHEW: On the night of May 17th, about three hundred men follow a lawyer named Thomas Dorr to the state arsenal in Providence. They bring cannon. The cannon don't fire. The attack falls apart.

3
00:00:23,468 --> 00:00:30,041
BARNABY THAYER: Bears, class is in session. And today's lesson, kids, is that a revolution needs working artillery.

4
00:00:30,341 --> 00:00:33,715
GARY MERCER: Two governors? How does a state end up with two governors?

5
00:00:34,015 --> 00:00:46,410
MATTHEW: Hold that, Gary. To get there, we start with a man who was governor three separate times, across thirty-eight years. James Fenner. He holds three numbers on Rhode Island's list. Seven, eleven and seventeen.

6
00:00:46,710 --> 00:00:57,182
MATTHEW: James Fenner was born in Providence on January 22nd, 1771. His father, Arthur Fenner, was Rhode Island's fourth governor and held the office for fifteen years, until he died in it.

7
00:00:57,482 --> 00:01:07,443
MATTHEW: James went up College Hill to the school then called Rhode Island College. He graduated in 1789. In 1804 the college took the name of a family of Providence merchants. Brown.

8
00:01:07,743 --> 00:01:12,062
BARNABY THAYER: Ah, the Browns. Now there's a tangent. I do love a tangent.

9
00:01:12,362 --> 00:01:29,995
MATTHEW: Take the tangent, Barnaby. In 2003 Brown's president, Ruth Simmons, set up a committee to study the university's ties to slavery. Its report came out in 2006. The Brown family profited partly from the transatlantic slave trade. John Brown defended it. His brother Moses became an abolitionist.

10
00:01:30,295 --> 00:01:34,694
BARNABY THAYER: To Brown's credit, it asked the question out loud. Most of the club never has.

11
00:01:34,994 --> 00:01:44,015
MATTHEW: Fenner rose fast, the way a governor's son does. He went to the United States Senate in 1805. Two years later he resigned the seat to come home and run for governor, and he won.

12
00:01:44,315 --> 00:01:55,590
MATTHEW: He served from 1807 to 1811 as a Democratic-Republican. He came back for another long run, 1824 to 1831. Then he stepped away. Rhode Island seemed to be done with him.

13
00:01:55,890 --> 00:02:14,252
MATTHEW: Now the count. Rhode Island's list starts in 1775 with Nicholas Cooke. Then William Greene, John Collins, Arthur Fenner. Arthur dies in office in 1805, and two short-term fill-ins, Henry Smith and Isaac Wilbour, each get a full number. That makes James Fenner number seven in 1807.

14
00:02:14,552 --> 00:02:27,349
MATTHEW: Rhode Island also hands out a fresh number every time someone comes back. That's how one man ends up with three of them. Count from statehood in 1790 instead, or skip the fill-ins, and number seven is someone else entirely.

15
00:02:27,649 --> 00:02:32,078
GARY MERCER: So in Rhode Island the same guy can be seventh, eleventh and seventeenth.

16
00:02:32,378 --> 00:02:35,097
BARNABY THAYER: Small state, Gary. We reuse everything.

17
00:02:35,397 --> 00:02:46,235
MATTHEW: Now, the two governors. In 1841 Rhode Island was still running under its colonial charter from 1663, a document signed by a king. To vote, you had to own land.

18
00:02:46,535 --> 00:02:55,875
MATTHEW: Factories had filled Providence with working men who owned nothing but their labor. By the 1840s, an estimated six in ten adult white men in Rhode Island could not vote.

19
00:02:56,175 --> 00:03:10,676
MATTHEW: So Thomas Dorr and his allies wrote their own People's Constitution, held their own vote, and in April 1842 elected Dorr governor. The old charter government re-elected Samuel Ward King. Two governments. One state.

20
00:03:10,976 --> 00:03:23,461
MATTHEW: The numbers tell it. In the 1840s Rhode Island had about twenty-three thousand adult white men. Fewer than ten thousand of them could vote. The rest were shut out by a rule written for a colony of farmers, in a state that was filling up with mills.

21
00:03:23,761 --> 00:03:40,769
MATTHEW: In December 1841 the People's Constitution went to its own unofficial vote. Nearly fourteen thousand men voted yes. Fifty-two voted no. Then in March 1842, the voters who already had the vote rejected a compromise constitution, eight thousand six hundred eighty-nine to eight thousand thirteen.

22
00:03:41,069 --> 00:03:58,209
MATTHEW: On April 18th, 1842, Dorr was elected governor, unopposed, with about sixty-three hundred votes. Two days later the charter government re-elected King with about forty-nine hundred. Dorr's men called their side the people. Dorr's critics called the charter government's harsh new law against them the Algerine Law.

23
00:03:58,509 --> 00:04:03,918
BARNABY THAYER: The Algerine Law. Named by its enemies, my friends, the way the best laws always are.

24
00:04:04,218 --> 00:04:09,554
GARY MERCER: So the guy with fewer votes stays governor, and the guy with more votes goes to prison.

25
00:04:09,854 --> 00:04:13,953
BARNABY THAYER: Welcome to Rhode Island in 1842, Gary. Bring a raincoat.

26
00:04:14,253 --> 00:04:25,847
MATTHEW: Then the arsenal. Then the collapse. Dorr was arrested, convicted of treason against Rhode Island in 1844, and sentenced to life. The legislature freed him in 1845.

27
00:04:26,147 --> 00:04:28,537
GARY MERCER: Life in prison? For wanting a vote?

28
00:04:28,837 --> 00:04:41,164
MATTHEW: Here is where the story gets more complicated than heroes and villains. The establishment answered with its own Law and Order Party. And to lead the state under a new constitution, it brought back an old man. James Fenner.

29
00:04:41,464 --> 00:04:53,353
MATTHEW: But look closely at Dorr's People's Constitution. It gave the vote to all white men. At its convention, a motion to strike the word white failed, eighteen to forty-six. The people's rebellion left Black men out.

30
00:04:53,653 --> 00:05:02,631
MATTHEW: The Law and Order constitution of 1842 did let Black men vote. It also kept a property test for immigrants. Mostly Irish, mostly Catholic, mostly the men in the mills.

31
00:05:02,931 --> 00:05:09,910
BARNABY THAYER: So the rebels shut out one group and the establishment shut out another. Nobody in that fight walks away clean, my friends.

32
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:16,212
MATTHEW: That's the lesson of Rhode Island. Every side drew a line around who counted. They just drew it in different places.

33
00:05:16,512 --> 00:05:27,591
MATTHEW: When the new constitution went to the voters in November 1842, it passed seven thousand and twenty-four to fifty-one. A separate question asked whether only white men should vote. The voters rejected it by about three to one.

34
00:05:27,891 --> 00:05:40,391
MATTHEW: Dorr was arrested in October 1843 and tried for treason against the state. He was convicted and refused to swear an oath of allegiance. In June 1845 the legislature passed an act that set him free.

35
00:05:40,691 --> 00:05:45,518
BARNABY THAYER: He refused to say the words. You can call that stubborn. I call it a thesis.

36
00:05:45,818 --> 00:05:58,950
MATTHEW: Fenner, meanwhile, had married Sarah Whipple Jenckes back in 1792. They had four children. He was the son of a governor, the husband of a Providence family, and a Brown man. He never had to ask permission to vote in his life.

37
00:05:59,250 --> 00:06:15,243
MATTHEW: In 1843 James Fenner became the first governor under the new constitution. He served until 1845. He died the next year, April 17th, 1846, at his Providence mansion, which he called What Cheer.

38
00:06:15,543 --> 00:06:19,651
MATTHEW: He is buried in the North Burial Ground in Providence, a short walk from College Hill.

39
00:06:19,951 --> 00:06:23,251
BARNABY THAYER: Three numbers on the list. Ever true. Thayer out.

40
00:06:23,551 --> 00:06:28,780
MATTHEW: Three numbers for one man, and for most of his life, most of his neighbors never got one vote.

41
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:31,952
MATTHEW: So, Rhode Island. Your lucky seven, one more time.

42
00:06:32,252 --> 00:06:38,465
MATTHEW: One. James Fenner was governor three times, so he is Rhode Island's 7th, 11th and 17th.

43
00:06:38,765 --> 00:06:43,673
MATTHEW: Two. Fenner graduated in 1789 from the college that became Brown University.

44
00:06:43,973 --> 00:06:47,892
MATTHEW: Three. He resigned a U.S. Senate seat in 1807 to become governor.

45
00:06:48,192 --> 00:06:51,829
MATTHEW: Four. Rhode Island gives every stint and every fill-in its own number.

46
00:06:52,129 --> 00:06:57,112
MATTHEW: Five. In the early 1840s about 60 percent of Rhode Island's adult white men could not vote.

47
00:06:57,412 --> 00:07:01,888
MATTHEW: Six. In 1843 Fenner returned as the Law and Order Party's governor.

48
00:07:02,188 --> 00:07:06,622
MATTHEW: Seven. Fenner was the first governor under Rhode Island's 1842 constitution.

49
00:07:06,922 --> 00:07:08,845
MATTHEW: Rhode Island. Keep it real.
